/** \addtogroup area_combinatorial */
/** \defgroup theory_combinatorial Theory of combinatorial algorithms
 \ingroup area_combinatorial

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/combinatorial` go beyond the book, after
 volume 4A of The Art of Computer Programming of Knuth: the broadword
 computation of his section 7.1.3, which works on the 64 bits of a word as on
 a vector of bits, and the generation of the basic combinatorial objects of
 his section 7.2.1 (the tuples, the permutations, the combinations, the
 partitions and the strings of parentheses), each in two versions, one as
 the text describes it and one engineered for speed. The enumeration by
 backtrack of chapter 16 of the book is instead in `include/ADS/backtrack`.
 This page collects what the headers take for granted: the definitions and
 the notation (see \ref theory_combinatorial_notation), the identities on
 which the broadword tricks rest (see \ref theory_combinatorial_broadword),
 the population count of arrays (see \ref theory_combinatorial_popcount),
 and the orders of the generation with the arguments behind their costs (see
 \ref theory_combinatorial_generation). The pseudocode of each algorithm is
 instead on the page of its header, in the comment of its function, together
 with the source that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_combinatorial_notation Definitions and notation

 A <em>word</em> \f$ x \f$ is an unsigned integer of 64 bits
 \f$ x = \sum_{k=0}^{63} x_k 2^k \f$, the bit \f$ x_k \f$ in the place
 \f$ k \f$, and the arithmetic on words is modulo \f$ 2^{64} \f$, so that
 \f$ -x = \lnot x + 1 \f$ (the complement plus 1). A word is also the set
 \f$ \{ k : x_k = 1 \} \subseteq \{ 0 , \ldots , 63 \} \f$, and the operations
 on the bits are those on the sets: \f$ \& \f$ the intersection,
 \f$ \mid \f$ the union, \f$ \oplus \f$ the symmetric difference.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \& \f$, \f$ \mid \f$, \f$ \oplus \f$, \f$ \lnot \f$</td>
     <td>the and, the or, the exclusive or and the complement of the bits,
         place by place</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ x \gg s \f$, \f$ x \ll s \f$</td>
     <td>the shifts by \f$ s \f$ places, i.e., \f$ \lfloor x / 2^s \rfloor \f$
         and \f$ 2^s x \bmod 2^{64} \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \nu( x ) \f$</td>
     <td>the sideways sum, the number of 1 bits of \f$ x \f$
         (<tt>sidewaysSum()</tt>)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \rho( x ) \f$, \f$ \lambda( x ) \f$</td>
     <td>the ruler function, the place of the lowest 1 (the trailing zeros),
         and the place of the highest 1, \f$ \lfloor \log_2 x \rfloor \f$, of
         \f$ x \ne 0 \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathtt{0x5555 \ldots 55} \f$</td>
     <td>a constant in hexadecimal with its byte repeated in the 8 bytes of
         the word</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \binom{n}{t} \f$, \f$ p( n ) \f$, \f$ \varpi_n \f$,
         \f$ C_n \f$</td>
     <td>the binomial coefficient, the partitions of the integer
         \f$ n \f$, the Bell number (the partitions of a set of \f$ n \f$)
         and the Catalan number \f$ \binom{2 n}{n} / ( n + 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_combinatorial_broadword Broadword computation

 The tricks of `Bitwise.h` replace a loop over the bits by a few operations
 on the whole word, each of which acts on all the places at once. Four
 identities are at their base. The lowest 1 of \f$ x \ne 0 \f$ is in the place
 \f$ \rho( x ) \f$, and \f$ x - 1 \f$ has the same bits above it, a 0 in it
 and 1s below it; hence
 \f[
   x \,\&\, -x = 2^{\rho( x )} , \quad
   x \mid ( x - 1 ) = x + 2^{\rho( x )} - 1 , \quad
   x \oplus ( x - 1 ) = 2^{\rho( x ) + 1} - 1 , \tag{1}
 \f]
 i.e., the lowest 1 alone (`rightmostOne()`), the 0s below it set
 (`smearRight()`), and a run of \f$ \rho( x ) + 1 \f$ ones, whose sideways
 sum gives `ruler()`.

 The sideways sum adds the bits in parallel: \f$ x - ( ( x \gg 1 ) \,\&\,
 \mathtt{0x5555 \ldots 55} ) \f$ holds in each field of 2 bits the number of
 its 1 bits (\f$ 2 a + b - a = a + b \f$), two masked shifts add the fields
 in pairs into fields of 4 and then of 8 bits, and the product by
 \f$ \mathtt{0x0101 \ldots 01} \f$ sums the 8 bytes into the top one, no sum
 exceeding 64 and hence no carry crossing a byte. The same doubling gives
 `lambda()` by halving the width of the search, `reverseBits()` by swapping
 the fields of 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 bits and the two halves, and `interleave()`,
 whose spread of 32 bits to the even places moves blocks of 16, 8, 4, 2 and
 1 bits; each of them costs \f$ O( \log w ) \f$ operations for a word of
 \f$ w \f$ bits.

 The Gray code \f$ g = x \oplus ( x \gg 1 ) \f$ of `gray()` changes by one
 bit from \f$ x \f$ to \f$ x + 1 \f$, since the carry flips the run of the
 lowest 1s of \f$ x \f$ and the bit above it, and the shift cancels all but
 one of the flips; its inverse is the prefix exclusive or
 \f$ x_k = \bigoplus_{j \ge k} g_j \f$, which `grayInverse()` computes by
 doubling the span, \f$ g \gets g \oplus ( g \gg s ) \f$ for
 \f$ s = 1 , 2 , 4 , \ldots , 32 \f$.

 <em>Lemma 1 (the trick of Gosper).</em> For \f$ x \ne 0 \f$, with
 \f$ u = x \,\&\, -x \f$ and \f$ v = x + u \f$, the next larger word with as
 many 1 bits as \f$ x \f$ is
 \f[
   v + ( ( ( v \oplus x ) / u ) \gg 2 ) . \tag{2}
 \f]

 Indeed, let the lowest run of 1s of \f$ x \f$ have \f$ r \f$ bits, from the
 place \f$ \rho( x ) \f$ up. The next word with as many 1s moves the top
 bit of that run one place up and the other \f$ r - 1 \f$ bits of the run to
 the bottom; \f$ v \f$ is \f$ x \f$ with the run cleared and the bit above it
 set, and \f$ v \oplus x \f$ is a run of \f$ r + 1 \f$ ones from the place
 \f$ \rho( x ) \f$, which the division by \f$ u \f$ brings to the place 0 and
 the shift by 2 shortens to \f$ r - 1 \f$ ones. `nextCombination()` of
 `FastGeneration.h` computes the same word without the division, from
 \f$ u = x \mid ( x - 1 ) \f$ and the shift by \f$ \rho( x ) + 1 \f$, the
 trailing zeros given by one instruction of the processor. Finally, the test
 of a zero byte of `hasZeroByte()` subtracts 1 from each byte: only a byte
 that is 0 borrows into its own top bit while having it clear in \f$ x \f$,
 and the lowest such byte is found exactly; a borrow from it may propagate a
 false 1 into a higher byte, which does not change the answer, since a zero
 byte exists in that case anyway.

 \section theory_combinatorial_popcount Population counts of arrays

 The 1 bits of an array of \f$ n \f$ words are the sum of their sideways
 sums, \f$ n \f$ instructions of population count on a processor that has
 one; `popcount()` of `FastBitwise.h` keeps four independent sums, so that
 the additions of consecutive words do not wait for one another. On the
 vectors of AVX2 and AVX-512 the method of Harley and Seal does fewer counts
 than words. A carry-save adder takes three vectors of bits \f$ a \f$,
 \f$ b \f$, \f$ c \f$ and gives, place by place,
 \f[
   \mathrm{sum} = a \oplus b \oplus c , \quad
   \mathrm{carry} = \mathrm{maj}( a , b , c ) , \quad
   a + b + c = \mathrm{sum} + 2 \, \mathrm{carry} , \tag{3}
 \f]
 so that a tree of 15 adders reduces a block of 16 vectors and the
 accumulators of the ones \f$ o \f$, of the twos \f$ t \f$, of the fours
 \f$ f \f$ and of the eights \f$ e \f$ to new accumulators and a vector
 \f$ s \f$ of the sixteens, by (3) with the weights of each level. Only the
 vector of the sixteens is counted, and the accumulators once at the end: the
 total is \f$ 16 \sum \nu( s ) \f$ over the blocks plus
 \f$ 8 \nu( e ) + 4 \nu( f ) + 2 \nu( t ) + \nu( o ) \f$, at the cost of one
 count and of 15 carry-save adders, i.e., a few dozen logical operations, per
 block of 16 vectors. A vector is counted by the instruction
 of AVX-512 if there is one, and otherwise by looking up the counts of its
 16 nibbles in a shuffle of bytes and adding the bytes of each word by
 `psadbw`. The rank of a bit, the population count of a prefix, is the base
 of `select()`, which finds the byte of the 1 of rank \f$ r \f$ by comparing
 \f$ r \f$ with the 8 prefix sums of the bytes in parallel, as in the
 zero-byte test, and then the bit inside the byte.

 \section theory_combinatorial_generation Generation of combinatorial objects

 A generator visits all the objects of a kind in a fixed order, each once,
 and its cost is measured per object, i.e., amortized over the whole
 sequence; a <em>loopless</em> one also bounds the work between two
 consecutive objects by a constant. The orders of `Generation.h` and of
 `FastGeneration.h` are the following.

 <table>
 <caption>The objects, their number and their order</caption>
 <tr><th>Objects</th><th>Number</th><th>Functions</th><th>Order</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$-tuples with \f$ 0 \le a_j < m_j \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ \prod_j m_j \f$</td>
     <td><tt>mixedRadix()</tt>, <tt>grayTuples()</tt></td>
     <td>lexicographic; reflected Gray, one place changed by 1</td></tr>
 <tr><td>permutations of a multiset</td>
     <td>\f$ n! / \prod_v m_v! \f$</td>
     <td><tt>permutationsLex()</tt>, <tt>forEachPermutationLex()</tt></td>
     <td>lexicographic</td></tr>
 <tr><td>permutations of \f$ n \f$ items</td><td>\f$ n! \f$</td>
     <td><tt>plainChanges()</tt>, <tt>heapPermutations()</tt>,
         <tt>forEachPermutation()</tt></td>
     <td>one adjacent swap; one swap</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ t \f$-combinations of \f$ n \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ \binom{n}{t} \f$</td>
     <td><tt>combinationsLex()</tt>, <tt>forEachCombination()</tt>,
         <tt>forEachCombinationMask()</tt>, <tt>revolvingDoor()</tt></td>
     <td>lexicographic; increasing as words; one item in and one
         out</td></tr>
 <tr><td>partitions of \f$ n \f$</td><td>\f$ p( n ) \f$</td>
     <td><tt>integerPartitions()</tt></td>
     <td>reverse lexicographic</td></tr>
 <tr><td>partitions of a set of \f$ n \f$</td><td>\f$ \varpi_n \f$</td>
     <td><tt>setPartitions()</tt></td>
     <td>lexicographic on the restricted growth strings</td></tr>
 <tr><td>strings of \f$ n \f$ pairs of parentheses</td><td>\f$ C_n \f$</td>
     <td><tt>nestedParentheses()</tt></td><td>lexicographic</td></tr>
 </table>

 The lexicographic successor has one scheme: find the last place that can
 grow, make it grow by the least amount, and set the places after it to
 their least values. For a permutation (Algorithm L of Narayana Pandita) the
 places after the last ascent \f$ a_j < a_{j+1} \f$ are nonincreasing, the
 least larger item of the suffix goes to the place \f$ j \f$, and the suffix
 is reversed. The scan of a suffix of length \f$ k \f$ happens once every
 \f$ k! \f$ permutations of distinct items; hence the work per permutation
 is at most proportional to \f$ \sum_{k \ge 1} k / k! = e \f$, a constant
 on the average though \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ in the worst case. The same
 argument gives \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized for the tuples, where a carry over
 the last \f$ k \f$ places happens once every
 \f$ m_{n-1} m_{n-2} \cdots m_{n-k} \ge 2^k \f$ tuples, and for the
 combinations. The engineered versions of `FastGeneration.h` remove the
 constant itself from the inner loop: the last place of a combination runs
 through its values by a loop of its own, the last 3 items of a permutation
 are written in their 6 orders directly, and the words of
 `forEachCombinationMask()` move their lowest 1 up by one addition while it
 can, (2) being needed only once per run.

 <em>Lemma 2 (Heap's method).</em> The iterative method of Heap, with a
 counter \f$ c_i < i \f$ for each place \f$ i \ge 1 \f$, swaps \f$ a_i \f$
 with \f$ a_{c_i} \f$ if \f$ i \f$ is odd and with \f$ a_0 \f$ if it is even,
 and visits the \f$ n! \f$ arrangements, each once, with one swap between
 two of them.

 Indeed, the counters run as a mixed-radix number with the radices
 \f$ 2 , 3 , \ldots , n \f$, i.e., through \f$ n! \f$ values, and Heap's
 choice of the swaps is the one that brings to the place \f$ i \f$ each of
 the items \f$ a_0 , \ldots , a_i \f$ once while the places below it run
 through all their arrangements. The place \f$ i \f$ changes once every
 \f$ i! \f$ arrangements, after the places below it have been reset;
 hence the work per arrangement of the counters is
 \f$ \sum_{i \ge 1} 1 / i! = e - 1 \f$, and unrolling the first 3 places,
 whose 6 arrangements are reached by the fixed swaps of the places 01, 02,
 01, 02, 01 (`forEachPermutation()`), leaves the counters a step every 6
 arrangements.

 The orders with one change between consecutive objects come from
 reflections. In the reflected Gray order of the tuples the last place runs
 up and down, and each other place changes when all the places after it are
 at an end of their range; Algorithm H keeps for each place its direction
 and a focus pointer to the next place to change, and so it is loopless. The
 plain changes of Steinhaus, Johnson and Trotter (Algorithm P) move the
 largest item from one end to the other through the arrangements of the
 others, recursively, with one adjacent swap per step. The revolving door of
 the combinations is the order \f$ R( n , t ) \f$ defined by
 \f[
   R( n , t ) = R( n - 1 , t ) , \;
                R( n - 1 , t - 1 )^{\mathrm{rev}} \cup \{ n - 1 \} ,
   \tag{4}
 \f]
 with \f$ R( n , 0 ) \f$ and \f$ R( t , t ) \f$ of one object; the last
 object of \f$ R( n - 1 , t ) \f$ and the first of the reversed second part
 differ by the item \f$ n - 1 \f$ in and one item out, and `revolvingDoor()`
 follows (4) recursively. Finally, the partitions of a set are in bijection
 with their <em>restricted growth strings</em>, \f$ a_j \f$ the block of the
 item \f$ j \f$, the blocks numbered in the order of their least items, so
 that \f$ a_0 = 0 \f$ and \f$ a_j \le 1 + \max( a_0 , \ldots , a_{j-1} ) \f$;
 and the strings of balanced parentheses, the words with as many opening as
 closing parentheses and no prefix with more closing than opening ones, are
 in bijection with the forests and with the binary trees of \f$ n \f$ nodes,
 which `nestedParentheses()` builds by a backtrack that tries the opening
 parenthesis first, a node of its tree costing \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ and every leaf
 of it being a solution.

